# Paths secretlint does not scan, each with a reason.
#
# FR-R3-109 — the exemption discipline is per ENTRY, never per directory. The scan this
# replaced skipped `tests/**` and `package-lock.json` wholesale, and that is precisely
# what made a real credential pasted into a test file invisible by construction. So
# nothing here excludes a source or test directory: every entry below is a generated or
# binary artifact where a finding could not be a real leak.
#
# Adding an entry means stating why a finding there would be a false positive. "It is
# noisy" is not a reason; a noisy true positive is still a leak.

# Generated coverage output. Reproduces byte-for-byte from the tests; any token-shaped
# string in it came from a fixture that is itself scanned.
coverage/**

# Build output for both trees. Derived from scanned sources; a secret here would be a
# finding in the source it was bundled from, which the scan reaches.
dist/**
out/**
webview-ui/dist/**

# Playwright and visual artifacts: screenshots and traces, binary or near-binary.
tests/visual/.artifacts/**
test-results/**
playwright-report/**

# The VSIX and its staging directory: a zip, plus a copy of already-scanned files.
*.vsix

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private-key REDACTION fixtures (FR-R3-109). Four files, each named, each with its
# reason — never `tests/**`, which is the exclusion that made a real credential
# invisible by construction.
#
# Why file scope here and value scope elsewhere. Two other findings in this tree were
# allowlisted by VALUE in `.secretlintrc.json` (`https://user:pass@example.com`, and a
# sequential-alphabet `ghp_` dummy), because those values are distinctive: a real
# credential would not match them. That is the granularity to prefer.
#
# It is not available for private keys. `secretlint-rule-privatekey` matches the
# **header** — `-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----` — and a real key has byte-identical
# headers to a fixture one. The distinguishing feature is the body, which the rule does
# not include in its match, so there is no value to allow that would not also allow
# every real key everywhere. File scope is the honest floor, and it is stated rather
# than presented as a preference.
#
# THE COMPENSATING CONTROL, because file scope is exactly how the old defect worked:
# `tests/lint/key-fixture-bodies-are-filler.test.ts` asserts every key block in these
# four files carries a sentinel or filler body and no plausible real key material. So a
# real key pasted into one of them fails a check even though secretlint no longer looks.

# The corpus that drives the redaction tests. Its whole purpose is to contain every
# key-header variant the redactor must recognise; bodies are `Filler`/sentinel strings.
tests/fixtures/key-block-corpus.ts

# Asserts the logger redacts key blocks. Cannot test that without containing them.
tests/unit/lib/key-block-redaction.test.ts

# The logger's own redaction tests: headers with elided bodies (`MIIEowIBAAKCAQEA…`).
tests/unit/lib/logger.test.ts

# One OPENSSH header, checking the transport sink redacts before it writes.
tests/unit/monitor/cli-transport-sink.test.ts
